---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { theoryClaims } from '../data/site-facts';
---

<Layout
  title="Third Enlightenment | Amitoj Singh"
  description={theoryClaims.summary}
>
  <section class="container-zen pt-8 pb-10 md:pt-12 md:pb-14">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <img
        src="/images/photography-optimized/DSCF0261.webp"
        alt="A car crossing a blurred landscape at sunset"
        class="w-full h-56 md:h-72 object-cover object-top mb-10"
        width="1600"
        height="1067"
      />

      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Working theory</p>
      <h1 class="mb-8 text-balance">Third Enlightenment</h1>
      <div class="accent-line mb-8"></div>
      <p class="text-lg text-ink-light">
        {theoryClaims.summary}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6 section-header">Observation</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-light mb-6">
        The system I use can hold thousands of notes, retrieve material I have forgotten, and propose connections among records I would not have placed beside one another.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-light">
        I still decide which proposed connection deserves attention. That division is the observation behind this theory.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6 section-header">Current claim</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-light">
        Awareness keeps a premise open to revision, while agency holds a direction long enough to act. The difficulty is preserving that relation as more cognition becomes delegable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6 section-header">Shadows</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-light">
        Uncertainty can keep every premise open until no direction is held long enough to act. Commitment carries a different risk: contradictory evidence can become noise while delegated cognition makes it easier to act on an inherited premise. At that point, direction has begun to close attention.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6 section-header">Limit</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-light">
        I do not yet know where the balance should sit. Attention to a premise can displace the commitment it was meant to improve, while delegation can make the boundary of human judgment harder to see. For now, the relation depends on what must remain revisable and what must be held long enough to act.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6 section-header">Stakes</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-light mb-6">
        In my use, the system can retrieve and compare more stored records than I can keep active at once, then propose a connection for inspection. Nothing in that operation requires the objective itself to be examined.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-light">
        The task I am trying to preserve therefore moves upstream: choosing what deserves attention, which direction to hold, and what I will not delegate.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Unresolved</p>
      <p class="text-2xl text-ink" data-theory-question>
        Can we act with full commitment while holding our premises lightly?
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
